insert into public.blog_posts (slug, title, excerpt, content, category_id, author_name, status, published_at, seo_title, seo_description, reading_minutes, cover_image_url, cover_image_alt) select 'how-to-choose-a-reliable-service-provider', 'How to Choose a Reliable Service Provider in South Africa', 'A practical checklist for hiring plumbers, electricians, mechanics and other service providers — from asking the right questions to getting quotes in writing.', $md$Whether your geyser has burst, your car is making a strange noise or you need someone to repaint the house, choosing the right service provider can save you money, time and a great deal of stress. The wrong choice can mean poor workmanship, hidden costs or a job that has to be redone.

This guide gives you a simple, repeatable process you can use for almost any service.

## 1. Be clear about what you need

Before you contact anyone, write down the problem or the job in a few sentences. Include:

- What needs to be done, and where.
- When you need it done, and whether it is urgent.
- Any photos that show the problem.
- Your rough budget, if you have one.

A clear brief helps businesses give you accurate quotes, and it makes it easier to compare them fairly.

## 2. Shortlist at least three businesses

Search for the service in your area and create a shortlist of two or three providers. Look at each business profile carefully:

- **Is the information complete?** Businesses that take the time to describe their services, list operating hours and add real photos of their work are usually more organised.
- **Do they serve your area?** Check the location and service areas so you don't pay unnecessary call-out fees.
- **Do they offer the specific service you need?** A general builder may not be the best choice for specialised waterproofing, for example.

## 3. Check qualifications and registrations

Many trades and professions in South Africa have formal registration requirements. Ask for proof where it applies to your job. For example:

- Electrical work on fixed installations should be done by a registered electrical contractor, who can issue a Certificate of Compliance (CoC) where required.
- Plumbers installing or replacing geysers should be able to provide the relevant plumbing certificate of compliance.
- Health professionals are generally registered with the relevant statutory council, such as the HPCSA.
- Building work on new homes may involve NHBRC requirements for the builder.

You don't need to be an expert in every regulation. Simply asking "Which body are you registered with, and can I see your certificate?" tells you a lot about how a business operates.

## 4. Ask the right questions

When you speak to each business, ask:

1. How long have you been doing this type of work?
2. Can you show me examples of similar jobs?
3. What exactly is included in the price, and what isn't?
4. Is there a call-out or assessment fee?
5. How long will the job take, and when can you start?
6. Do you offer a workmanship guarantee? For how long?
7. How do you prefer to be paid, and when?

Pay attention to how clearly and patiently they answer. Good communication before the job usually means good communication during it.

## 5. Always get a written quote

A verbal estimate is easy to forget or dispute. Ask for a written quote — even a WhatsApp message or email is better than nothing — that lists:

- The work to be done, item by item.
- Materials and their cost.
- Labour cost.
- VAT, if the business is VAT-registered.
- Payment terms and the expected completion date.

Compare quotes line by line rather than only looking at the total. A very low quote can mean lower-quality materials or work that has been left out.

## 6. Be careful with deposits

Some businesses reasonably ask for a deposit to buy materials. Protect yourself by:

- Keeping deposits proportionate to the cost of materials.
- Paying into a business bank account rather than cash where possible.
- Getting a written acknowledgement of every payment.
- Never paying the full amount before the work starts.

If someone pressures you to pay everything upfront "today only", treat it as a warning sign.

## 7. Watch for red flags

Be cautious if a provider:

- Refuses to give a written quote.
- Cannot give a physical address or a consistent contact number.
- Arrives without the tools or materials they said they'd bring.
- Pressures you to make a decision immediately.
- Asks you to pay into a personal account that doesn't match the business name.

## 8. Keep a record of everything

Save quotes, messages, invoices, certificates and photos of the work before and after. If something goes wrong, a clear paper trail makes it much easier to resolve the issue with the business directly or, if necessary, to approach the relevant ombud or the National Consumer Commission.

## Putting it into practice

Choosing a service provider doesn't need to be complicated. Define the job, compare a few well-presented businesses, ask direct questions, get everything in writing and pay sensibly. On Findora you can compare business profiles, services and operating hours, then call, WhatsApp or send an enquiry directly — so you stay in control of the conversation from the start.
$md$, c.id, 'Findora Editorial Team', 'published', now() - interval '60 days', 'How to Choose a Reliable Service Provider in South Africa', 'A practical checklist for hiring plumbers, electricians, mechanics and other service providers — from asking the right questions to getting quotes in writing.', 4, '/blog/covers/how-to-choose-a-reliable-service-provider.webp', 'Illustration for the article: How to Choose a Reliable Service Provider in South Africa' from public.blog_categories c where c.slug = 'finding-services' on conflict (slug) do nothing;
update public.blog_posts set cover_image_url = '/blog/covers/how-to-choose-a-reliable-service-provider.webp', cover_image_alt = 'Illustration for the article: How to Choose a Reliable Service Provider in South Africa' where slug = 'how-to-choose-a-reliable-service-provider' and cover_image_url is null;
insert into public.blog_posts (slug, title, excerpt, content, category_id, author_name, status, published_at, seo_title, seo_description, reading_minutes, cover_image_url, cover_image_alt) select 'starting-a-small-business-in-south-africa', 'Starting a Small Business in South Africa: A Step-by-Step Guide', 'From testing your idea to registering with CIPC and SARS, opening a business bank account and finding your first customers — a practical roadmap for new entrepreneurs.', $md$Starting a business is one of the most rewarding things you can do, and South Africa has no shortage of entrepreneurs solving real problems in their communities. The first few months, however, can feel overwhelming. This guide breaks the process into clear steps so you know what to do first, what can wait, and where to get reliable information.

*This article is general guidance, not legal or tax advice. Requirements change, so confirm details with the relevant authority or a qualified professional.*

## Step 1: Test your idea before you spend money

The most expensive mistake new businesses make is building something nobody wants to pay for. Before registering anything:

- **Talk to potential customers.** Ask what they currently do to solve the problem and what frustrates them about it.
- **Check the competition.** Search your area for similar businesses. Competition is not bad — it proves there is demand — but you need a reason for customers to choose you.
- **Make a small first sale.** Offer your service to a few people, even at a discount, and learn from the experience.

## Step 2: Choose a business structure

The most common structures for small businesses are:

- **Sole proprietor.** You trade in your own name or a trading name. It is simple and inexpensive, but there is no legal separation between you and the business.
- **Private company — (Pty) Ltd.** Registered with the Companies and Intellectual Property Commission (CIPC). The company is a separate legal entity, which can make it easier to open accounts, tender for work and bring in partners.
- **Non-profit company (NPC)** for organisations whose purpose is public benefit rather than profit.

Many entrepreneurs start as sole proprietors and register a company once the business grows. An accountant can help you decide which structure suits your situation.

## Step 3: Register with CIPC (if you choose a company)

Company registration is done online through CIPC. You will typically:

1. Reserve a company name (or register with a number-based name).
2. Complete the registration with the details of the directors.
3. Receive your registration certificate and company registration number.

Keep your registration documents safe — banks, clients and suppliers will ask for them. Remember that companies must also file annual returns with CIPC.

## Step 4: Get your tax affairs in order

Every business has tax obligations. Registered companies are generally registered for income tax with the South African Revenue Service (SARS). Sole proprietors declare business income on their personal tax return.

Other registrations may apply depending on your turnover and whether you employ people, for example VAT registration once you reach the compulsory threshold, and PAYE, UIF and SDL when you have employees. A registered tax practitioner can help you understand what applies to you.

## Step 5: Open a separate business bank account

Mixing personal and business money makes it very hard to know whether you are actually making a profit, and it creates headaches at tax time. Open a business account as soon as possible and:

- Pay all business income into it.
- Pay all business expenses from it.
- Pay yourself a regular, fixed amount rather than withdrawing money whenever you need it.

## Step 6: Understand the rules for your industry

Some sectors have specific registration or licensing requirements — for example food businesses, health professionals, financial advisors, security providers, electricians and builders. Find out early whether your trade needs a licence, certificate or professional registration, and budget for it.

## Step 7: Price for profit, not just for sales

Work out what it actually costs you to deliver your product or service, including:

- Materials or stock.
- Transport and fuel.
- Data, airtime and equipment.
- Your own time.

Then add a profit margin. Many new businesses underprice to win customers and end up working hard for very little. It is easier to offer a discount later than to raise prices for existing customers.

## Step 8: Make your business easy to find

Customers can't support a business they can't find. At minimum:

- Create a professional business profile on directories such as Findora, with a clear description, your services and real photos.
- Set up a WhatsApp Business account with your operating hours and a catalogue.
- Ask satisfied customers to recommend you to friends and family.

## Step 9: Keep simple records from day one

Record every sale and expense, keep invoices and receipts, and reconcile your bank account every month. Even a spreadsheet works. Good records help you make decisions, apply for funding and stay compliant.

## Step 10: Look for support

South Africa has many support programmes for small businesses, including government agencies, enterprise development programmes run by larger companies, incubators and local business chambers. Speak to other entrepreneurs in your area too — they often know which programmes are genuinely useful.

## The bottom line

Start small, test your idea, register properly, separate your finances and make it easy for customers to reach you. Every established business you admire started with the same first steps.
