insert into public.blog_posts (slug, title, excerpt, content, category_id, author_name, status, published_at, seo_title, seo_description, reading_minutes, cover_image_url, cover_image_alt) select 'choosing-professional-services', 'Choosing an Accountant, Attorney or Consultant: What to Look For', 'Professional services involve trust, confidentiality and expertise. How to evaluate qualifications, fees, communication and fit before you appoint an accountant, attorney or consultant.', $md$Hiring a professional — an accountant, attorney, financial advisor, architect or business consultant — is different from hiring a tradesperson. You are paying for expertise and judgement, often on matters that are confidential or have long-term consequences. A good choice can protect you from costly mistakes; a poor one can create them.

Here is how to make a confident decision.

## Define the problem you need solved

"I need an accountant" is a starting point, but be more specific:

- Do you need monthly bookkeeping, annual financial statements, tax returns, or payroll?
- Is your legal matter a property transfer, a labour dispute, a contract review or an estate?
- Are you looking for a once-off project or an ongoing relationship?

Professionals often specialise. A clear description helps you find the right specialist and get a more accurate fee estimate.

## Check registrations and professional membership

Many professions in South Africa are regulated or have recognised professional bodies. Before appointing someone, ask which body they belong to and confirm their status where a public register is available. For example:

- Attorneys are admitted by the High Court and regulated by the Legal Practice Council.
- Tax practitioners must be registered with SARS and a recognised controlling body.
- Accountants may be members of professional bodies such as SAICA, SAIPA or others.
- Financial advisors should be authorised by, or represent a provider authorised by, the Financial Sector Conduct Authority (FSCA).

Membership doesn't guarantee a perfect fit, but it means the professional has met certain standards and is accountable to a regulatory or disciplinary process.

## Ask about relevant experience

Ask how often they handle matters like yours and whether they work with businesses or individuals similar to you. A conveyancer who processes many transfers in your area, or an accountant familiar with your industry, will usually work faster and anticipate issues better.

## Understand how fees work

Professional fees can be structured in different ways:

- **Hourly rates**, common for legal and consulting work.
- **Fixed fees** for clearly defined tasks, such as a tax return or a simple will.
- **Monthly retainers** for ongoing bookkeeping or advisory services.
- **Fees set by tariff or guideline** in certain regulated areas.

Ask for a written fee estimate or engagement letter that explains what is included, how extra work is charged and when payment is due. If a fee seems unusually low or high, ask why.

## Evaluate communication

During your first conversation, notice:

- Do they explain things in language you understand?
- Do they ask thoughtful questions about your situation?
- Do they tell you honestly what they can and cannot do?
- How quickly do they respond?

You will be working closely with this person. Clear communication and mutual respect matter.

## Confidentiality and data protection

Professionals handle sensitive personal and financial information. Ask how they store documents, who in their office will have access, and how they share files with you. Responsible firms take the Protection of Personal Information Act (POPIA) seriously and will be comfortable answering these questions.

## Watch for warning signs

Be cautious if a professional:

- Guarantees an outcome that depends on a court, regulator or market.
- Pressures you to sign immediately.
- Is vague about fees or refuses to put them in writing.
- Suggests ways to avoid legal obligations that sound too good to be true.
- Cannot confirm their registration.

## Start with a defined first task

If you are unsure, begin with a smaller, clearly defined piece of work. It is a low-risk way to experience how the professional works before committing to a longer relationship.

## Using Findora to compare professionals

On Findora you can browse categories such as Legal Services, Financial Services and Professional Services, filter by location, review services and descriptions, and contact providers directly by phone, WhatsApp or enquiry. Use the questions in this guide during that first conversation, and you'll be well placed to choose a professional you can trust.
$md$, c.id, 'Findora Editorial Team', 'published', now() - interval '42 days', 'Choosing an Accountant, Attorney or Consultant: What to Look For', 'Professional services involve trust, confidentiality and expertise. How to evaluate qualifications, fees, communication and fit before you appoint an accountant, attorney', 3, '/blog/covers/choosing-professional-services.webp', 'Illustration for the article: Choosing an Accountant, Attorney or Consultant: What to Look For' from public.blog_categories c where c.slug = 'finding-services' on conflict (slug) do nothing;
update public.blog_posts set cover_image_url = '/blog/covers/choosing-professional-services.webp', cover_image_alt = 'Illustration for the article: Choosing an Accountant, Attorney or Consultant: What to Look For' where slug = 'choosing-professional-services' and cover_image_url is null;
insert into public.blog_posts (slug, title, excerpt, content, category_id, author_name, status, published_at, seo_title, seo_description, reading_minutes, cover_image_url, cover_image_alt) select 'technology-tools-for-small-businesses', 'Affordable Technology Tools Every Small Business Should Consider', 'The right tools save hours every week. A practical overview of affordable technology for communication, invoicing, bookings, payments, file storage and security.', $md$Technology should make running your business easier, not more complicated. You don't need expensive systems to work efficiently — many effective tools are free or cost less than a weekly fuel top-up. This guide covers the categories that matter most for small businesses and what to look for in each.

## 1. Business communication

**WhatsApp Business** is the starting point for many South African businesses. Useful features include:

- A business profile with your address, hours and website.
- Quick replies for common questions.
- Labels to organise customers (new enquiry, quoted, paid).
- A catalogue of products or services.

A **professional email address** using your own domain (for example info@yourbusiness.co.za) looks more credible than a free personal address and is inexpensive to set up with most hosting providers.

## 2. Quoting and invoicing

Handwritten invoices get lost and look unprofessional. Cloud accounting and invoicing tools let you:

- Create branded quotes and invoices in minutes.
- Track which invoices are unpaid.
- Send automatic payment reminders.
- Keep records ready for your accountant and for SARS.

Many offer free or low-cost plans for very small businesses. Choose one that your accountant is comfortable working with.

## 3. Bookings and scheduling

If you work by appointment — salons, consultants, tutors, health practitioners — an online booking tool can reduce back-and-forth messages. Look for:

- Automatic reminders to reduce no-shows.
- Calendar sync with your phone.
- The option to take deposits for certain services.

Even a shared digital calendar is a big step up from a paper diary.

## 4. Accepting payments

Customers increasingly expect to pay by card or instant EFT. Options include card machines that connect to your phone, payment links you can send by WhatsApp or email, and QR code payments. Compare transaction fees, settlement times and monthly costs before choosing.

## 5. File storage and backups

Store important documents — registration certificates, contracts, quotes, invoices and photos — in a cloud storage service rather than only on one phone or laptop. If a device is lost or stolen, your records are safe.

Organise files into simple folders (Clients, Suppliers, Finance, Compliance) and name files consistently so you can find them quickly.

## 6. Productivity and collaboration

If you work with a team, shared documents, spreadsheets and task lists help everyone stay on the same page. A simple shared list of jobs with statuses ("to quote", "booked", "in progress", "invoiced") can replace dozens of messages.

## 7. Online presence

Your online presence is also a technology tool:

- A complete Findora business profile that customers can find by service and location.
- A Google Business Profile for map searches.
- A simple website if your business benefits from more detailed information.

On Findora, your dashboard also shows real statistics — profile views, call and WhatsApp clicks, and enquiries — so you can see which efforts are working.

## 8. Cybersecurity basics

Small businesses are targets for scams and account takeovers. Protect yourself with a few habits:

- **Use strong, unique passwords** and a password manager.
- **Turn on two-factor authentication** for email, banking, social media and WhatsApp.
- **Be suspicious of urgent payment requests**, especially changed banking details sent by email. Phone the supplier on a known number to confirm.
- **Keep devices updated** with the latest software.
- **Back up regularly.**

## 9. Choosing tools wisely

Before signing up for anything, ask:

1. What problem does this solve, and how much time will it save?
2. What does it cost per month, including transaction fees?
3. Is it easy to cancel, and can I export my data?
4. Does it work well on a phone?
5. How does the provider protect my and my customers' data?

Start with one tool, use it properly for a month, then add the next.

## Final thought

The best technology is the technology you actually use. Choose simple tools that remove repetitive work, keep your records organised and help customers reach you — then spend the time you save on growing your business.
$md$, c.id, 'Findora Editorial Team', 'published', now() - interval '39 days', 'Affordable Technology Tools Every Small Business Should Consider', 'The right tools save hours every week. A practical overview of affordable technology for communication, invoicing, bookings, payments, file storage and security.', 3, '/blog/covers/technology-tools-for-small-businesses.webp', 'Illustration for the article: Affordable Technology Tools Every Small Business Should Consider' from public.blog_categories c where c.slug = 'business-technology' on conflict (slug) do nothing;
update public.blog_posts set cover_image_url = '/blog/covers/technology-tools-for-small-businesses.webp', cover_image_alt = 'Illustration for the article: Affordable Technology Tools Every Small Business Should Consider' where slug = 'technology-tools-for-small-businesses' and cover_image_url is null;
insert into public.blog_posts (slug, title, excerpt, content, category_id, author_name, status, published_at, seo_title, seo_description, reading_minutes, cover_image_url, cover_image_alt) select 'optimise-your-business-profile', 'How to Optimise Your Findora Business Profile for More Enquiries', 'A step-by-step guide to writing a strong description, choosing categories, listing services, adding photos and keeping your profile up to date so customers choose you.', $md$Your business profile often creates the first impression a customer has of your business. In a few seconds they decide whether to call you or move on to the next listing. This guide walks through each part of your Findora profile and how to make it work harder for you.

## 1. Choose the right category and subcategory

Customers often browse by category, and search results consider your category and subcategory. Choose the option that best describes your **main** service. If you offer several types of service, use the services section to cover the others — each service can be assigned its own service category.

## 2. Write a clear, specific description

Your description should answer four questions in the first few sentences:

1. **What do you do?**
2. **Who do you do it for?**
3. **Where do you operate?**
4. **Why should customers choose you?**

Compare these two descriptions:

> "We provide quality services at affordable prices. Contact us today."

> "Family-run plumbing business serving Klerksdorp, Orkney and Stilfontein since 2012. We install and repair geysers, fix leaks and unblock drains for homes and small businesses, with a 12-month workmanship guarantee."

The second one is specific, builds trust and includes words customers actually search for.

Keep paragraphs short, avoid capital letters for emphasis, and don't copy your description from another business.

## 3. Add a short tagline

The tagline appears near your name and in search results. Use it to state your speciality in one line, for example "Emergency electrical repairs in Mahikeng — 7 days a week."

## 4. List every important service

Services help customers understand exactly what you offer and help your profile appear in more searches. For each service:

- Use a clear name customers would recognise ("Wheel alignment", not "WA service").
- Add a one- or two-sentence description.
- Choose a price type: fixed, starting from, hourly, quotation required, or free.
- Add availability if relevant ("Weekdays, by appointment").

You don't have to publish prices. However, "starting from" prices help customers decide whether to contact you and reduce enquiries that don't fit your pricing.

## 5. Upload a logo and cover photo

Your logo appears in search results and on your profile, so make sure it is clear at a small size. Use a square image with a plain background where possible.

Your cover photo sits at the top of your profile. Choose a wide, high-quality image that represents your business — your shopfront, team, vehicle or a strong example of your work.

## 6. Build a gallery of real photos

Add photos that show:

- Completed work (before and after is ideal).
- Your premises or workspace.
- Equipment, products or vehicles.
- Your team at work.

Write a short description for each photo. Descriptions help customers who use screen readers and give search engines more context.

## 7. Complete your contact details and hours

Add every channel you actively monitor — phone, WhatsApp, email and website — and your social media links. Then set your operating hours. Customers are more likely to contact a business when they know it's open.

Only list numbers you answer. An unanswered phone can cost you more than a missing one.

## 8. Get your location right

Select your province and city, add your suburb, and list the other areas you serve. If you work from home, you can hide your street address while still appearing in searches for your area. Adding map coordinates enables the "Get Directions" button for customers who visit you.

## 9. Respond to enquiries quickly

When a customer submits an enquiry, you'll receive an email and see it in your dashboard. Fast, friendly responses dramatically increase your chances of winning the job. Mark enquiries as read and archive them when they're dealt with to keep your inbox manageable.

## 10. Review your statistics monthly

Your dashboard shows profile views, phone clicks, WhatsApp clicks, website clicks and enquiries. Use them to improve:

- **Few views?** Check your category, service names and location details.
- **Views but few contacts?** Improve your description, photos and pricing information.
- **Many calls but few enquiries?** That's fine — customers are reaching you directly.

## 11. Keep it current

Update your profile whenever your hours, services, prices or contact details change. An accurate profile builds trust; an outdated one frustrates customers.

## Quick checklist

- [ ] Correct category and subcategory
- [ ] Specific description and tagline
- [ ] All main services listed
- [ ] Logo, cover photo and at least six gallery photos
- [ ] Phone, WhatsApp and/or email
- [ ] Operating hours
- [ ] Location and service areas

A complete, honest and up-to-date profile is one of the most effective free marketing tools available to your business.
$md$, c.id, 'Findora Editorial Team', 'published', now() - interval '36 days', 'How to Optimise Your Findora Business Profile for More Enquiries', 'A step-by-step guide to writing a strong description, choosing categories, listing services, adding photos and keeping your profile up to date so customers choose you.', 4, '/blog/covers/optimise-your-business-profile.webp', 'Illustration for the article: How to Optimise Your Findora Business Profile for More Enquiries' from public.blog_categories c where c.slug = 'business-growth' on conflict (slug) do nothing;
update public.blog_posts set cover_image_url = '/blog/covers/optimise-your-business-profile.webp', cover_image_alt = 'Illustration for the article: How to Optimise Your Findora Business Profile for More Enquiries' where slug = 'optimise-your-business-profile' and cover_image_url is null;
insert into public.blog_posts (slug, title, excerpt, content, category_id, author_name, status, published_at, seo_title, seo_description, reading_minutes, cover_image_url, cover_image_alt) select 'building-customer-trust', 'Building Customer Trust: Practical Steps for Small Businesses', 'Trust is the foundation of every sale. Learn how transparency, consistency, professionalism and honest handling of problems help customers feel confident choosing your business.', $md$People buy from businesses they trust. That's especially true for services, where customers often can't judge quality until the job is done. Large brands build trust with advertising budgets; small businesses build it through hundreds of small, consistent actions.

Here are practical ways to earn — and keep — your customers' confidence.

## Be transparent about who you are

Customers want to know they're dealing with a real, accountable business:

- Use your actual business name consistently.
- Show where you're based or which areas you serve.
- Introduce the people behind the business — even a short "about us" helps.
- If you're registered with CIPC, a professional body or an industry association, say so (and be ready to show proof).

On Findora you can choose to display your business registration number on your profile. Only claim registrations you actually hold.

## Be clear about prices

Uncertainty about cost is one of the biggest reasons customers hesitate. Where possible:

- Publish "starting from" prices or price ranges for common services.
- Explain call-out or assessment fees upfront.
- Provide written, itemised quotes.
- Tell customers immediately if the scope changes and the price will be affected — before doing the extra work.

## Show real evidence of your work

Photos of real jobs, examples of previous projects and honest descriptions of your experience are more convincing than claims like "best in town". Never use other businesses' photos or invent achievements.

## Communicate reliably

Trust grows when you do what you say:

- Arrive when you said you would, or let the customer know in advance if you'll be late.
- Return calls and messages promptly.
- Explain what you're doing and why.
- Confirm agreements in writing.

Small acts of reliability add up to a reputation.

## Handle mistakes honestly

Every business makes mistakes. What matters is how you respond. Acknowledge the problem, apologise sincerely, fix it properly and learn from it. Customers often become more loyal after seeing a problem handled well.

## Offer a guarantee you can honour

A workmanship guarantee — for example, returning to fix any defect in your work within a set period at no charge — reduces the customer's risk. Make the terms clear and realistic, and always honour them.

## Protect customer information

Customers share names, numbers, addresses and sometimes sensitive details with you. Treat that information with care:

- Use it only to provide the service and communicate about it.
- Don't share it with others without permission.
- Store it securely and delete what you no longer need.
- Don't send marketing messages unless customers have agreed.

Respecting privacy is both a legal obligation under POPIA and a powerful trust signal.

## Make it easy to reach you — before and after the sale

A business that disappears after receiving payment quickly loses trust. Keep the same contact numbers, respond to after-sales questions and be easy to find. A complete, up-to-date directory profile helps customers find you again when they need you.

## Be consistent across every channel

Your profile, WhatsApp, social media, vehicle branding and invoices should all show the same name, logo and contact details. Consistency signals stability and professionalism.

## Avoid shortcuts that damage trust

Some tactics may bring short-term attention but cause long-term harm:

- Fake reviews or testimonials.
- Misleading "verified" or award claims.
- Hidden fees.
- High-pressure sales tactics.
- Copying other businesses' content.

Findora doesn't allow fake reviews or misleading verification claims on listings, and verification badges are only shown after a real verification process.

## Trust is built over time

There's no shortcut to a trusted reputation, but every honest quote, punctual arrival and well-handled problem moves you closer. Customers notice — and they tell others.
$md$, c.id, 'Findora Editorial Team', 'published', now() - interval '33 days', 'Building Customer Trust: Practical Steps for Small Businesses', 'Trust is the foundation of every sale. Learn how transparency, consistency, professionalism and honest handling of problems help customers feel confident choosing your bu', 3, '/blog/covers/building-customer-trust.webp', 'Illustration for the article: Building Customer Trust: Practical Steps for Small Businesses' from public.blog_categories c where c.slug = 'business-growth' on conflict (slug) do nothing;
update public.blog_posts set cover_image_url = '/blog/covers/building-customer-trust.webp', cover_image_alt = 'Illustration for the article: Building Customer Trust: Practical Steps for Small Businesses' where slug = 'building-customer-trust' and cover_image_url is null;
insert into public.blog_posts (slug, title, excerpt, content, category_id, author_name, status, published_at, seo_title, seo_description, reading_minutes, cover_image_url, cover_image_alt) select 'supporting-local-economic-development', 'How Small Businesses Drive Local Economic Development', 'Small enterprises are central to jobs and opportunity in South African towns and townships. How local economic development works, and practical ways residents, businesses and organisations can strengthen it.', $md$Local economic development (LED) is about improving the economic well-being of a specific place — a town, a township, a district — by building on its own people, businesses and resources. In South Africa, municipalities have a role in creating an environment where local economies can grow, but much of the real activity happens through small businesses, entrepreneurs and the customers who support them.

## What local economic development looks like

Strong local economies tend to share some features:

- A variety of businesses, so the area isn't dependent on a single employer.
- Opportunities for local people to work, train and start enterprises.
- Infrastructure that supports trade — roads, electricity, connectivity and safe public spaces.
- Networks that connect businesses with customers, suppliers and each other.
- Money that circulates within the community rather than immediately leaving it.

## Why small businesses matter so much

Small, medium and micro enterprises (SMMEs) and informal businesses provide livelihoods for a large number of South Africans. They often:

- Employ people who live nearby.
- Buy from other local suppliers.
- Provide services that larger companies don't offer in smaller towns.
- Adapt quickly to local needs.
- Train young people through practical experience and apprenticeships.

When these businesses grow, the benefits spread through the community.

## The barriers small businesses face

Despite their importance, many small enterprises struggle with:

- **Visibility** — customers simply don't know they exist.
- **Access to finance** — limited credit history or collateral.
- **Access to markets** — difficulty reaching customers outside their immediate area or winning contracts from larger organisations.
- **Skills and information** — on compliance, marketing, finance or technology.
- **Costs** — transport, data, rent and electricity.

No single solution addresses all of these, but small improvements in each area add up.

## How residents can help

Individuals have more influence than they might think:

1. **Search for local options first** when you need a product or service.
2. **Pay promptly**, especially for small businesses with limited cash flow.
3. **Recommend good businesses** to friends, family and community groups.
4. **Give constructive feedback** directly to the business so it can improve.

## How businesses can help each other

- **Buy from each other.** A local caterer can use a local bakery; a construction company can hire a local electrician.
- **Refer customers** to trusted complementary businesses.
- **Share knowledge** through informal networks, chambers or WhatsApp groups.
- **Mentor newcomers** where you have experience to share.

## How larger organisations can help

Corporates, schools, churches, NGOs and government departments can make a big difference through local procurement — deliberately sourcing goods and services from businesses in the area — and through enterprise and supplier development programmes that offer training, mentorship and access to markets.

## The role of digital discovery

As more South Africans search for services on their phones, being discoverable online is becoming a basic requirement. Yet many capable local businesses remain invisible because they don't have a website or marketing budget.

Free business directories help close that gap. When a business can list its services, location, hours and contact details in one structured, searchable place, customers from across the town — and from neighbouring towns — can find it. Search data can also reveal unmet demand: services people search for but can't find locally, which may represent opportunities for new businesses.

## A shared responsibility

Local economic development isn't only a government programme. It happens every time a resident chooses a local provider, a business refers work to a neighbour or an organisation buys locally. Findora aims to make those choices easier by helping South Africans discover and connect with the businesses in their own communities.
$md$, c.id, 'Findora Editorial Team', 'published', now() - interval '30 days', 'How Small Businesses Drive Local Economic Development', 'Small enterprises are central to jobs and opportunity in South African towns and townships. How local economic development works, and practical ways residents, businesses', 3, '/blog/covers/supporting-local-economic-development.webp', 'Illustration for the article: How Small Businesses Drive Local Economic Development' from public.blog_categories c where c.slug = 'local-economy' on conflict (slug) do nothing;
update public.blog_posts set cover_image_url = '/blog/covers/supporting-local-economic-development.webp', cover_image_alt = 'Illustration for the article: How Small Businesses Drive Local Economic Development' where slug = 'supporting-local-economic-development' and cover_image_url is null;
insert into public.blog_posts (slug, title, excerpt, content, category_id, author_name, status, published_at, seo_title, seo_description, reading_minutes, cover_image_url, cover_image_alt) select 'how-to-avoid-service-scams', 'How to Spot and Avoid Common Service Scams', 'Fake tradespeople, advance-fee tricks and changed banking details cost South Africans money every year. Learn the warning signs and simple habits that protect you.', $md$Most service providers are honest people trying to earn a living. Unfortunately, a minority of scammers take advantage of customers who need help quickly. Knowing the common tactics makes it much harder for them to succeed.

## Common scams to watch for

### The upfront payment scam

A "business" offers an unusually good price, then asks for full or large upfront payment — for materials, a "booking fee" or a "special discount that ends today". Once paid, they stop responding.

**Protect yourself:** Pay deposits only in proportion to genuine material costs, ideally directly to a supplier or into a business account that matches the business name. Never pay the full amount before work begins.

### Changed banking details

You receive an email or message, apparently from a business you've dealt with, saying their bank details have changed. The new account belongs to a fraudster.

**Protect yourself:** Always confirm changed banking details by phoning the business on a number you already know — not one supplied in the message.

### The fake listing or copied identity

Scammers copy the name, logo or photos of a real business and advertise under it with a different phone number.

**Protect yourself:** Check that contact details match across the business's profiles. Be cautious if a business's photos look like stock images or appear under different business names. On Findora, every listing is reviewed before publication, and you can report suspicious listings from any profile.

### The "unnecessary repairs" tactic

A provider inspects your geyser, car or roof and claims that extensive, urgent repairs are needed — far beyond the original problem.

**Protect yourself:** For large or unexpected repairs, ask for a written explanation and photos, then get a second quote before agreeing.

### Unrequested door-to-door services

Someone arrives unannounced offering to fix a problem they claim to have noticed, often demanding payment on the spot.

**Protect yourself:** Don't let unexpected visitors into your home. Take their details and verify the business independently before agreeing to anything.

### Overpayment and refund requests

A "customer" or "supplier" pays you more than agreed and asks you to refund the difference. The original payment later reverses.

**Protect yourself:** Wait for funds to clear fully in your account before refunding anything, and be suspicious of overpayments.

## General warning signs

Be cautious when someone:

- Pressures you to decide or pay immediately.
- Offers a price far below other quotes.
- Avoids giving a written quote, address or registration details.
- Insists on cash or untraceable payment methods.
- Uses a personal bank account in a different name.
- Gives inconsistent information about their business.

## Good habits that protect you

1. **Get at least two quotes** for significant work.
2. **Ask for proof** of relevant registrations and certificates.
3. **Get everything in writing** — quote, scope, timeline, payment terms.
4. **Keep records** of messages, invoices and payments.
5. **Pay in stages** linked to completed work.
6. **Trust your instincts.** If something feels wrong, pause.

## If something goes wrong

- Stop any further payments immediately.
- Contact your bank as soon as possible if you paid electronically.
- Report fraud to the South African Police Service and obtain a case number.
- Report the listing on the platform where you found the business — on Findora, use "Report this listing" on the business profile.
- For disputes with legitimate businesses, consider the relevant industry ombud or the National Consumer Commission.

## Staying safe while supporting local business

Scams shouldn't stop you from supporting local businesses. By comparing profiles, asking the right questions and paying sensibly, you can enjoy the benefits of local services while keeping your money safe. If you spot a suspicious listing on Findora, please report it — every report helps keep the platform trustworthy for everyone.
$md$, c.id, 'Findora Editorial Team', 'published', now() - interval '27 days', 'How to Spot and Avoid Common Service Scams', 'Fake tradespeople, advance-fee tricks and changed banking details cost South Africans money every year. Learn the warning signs and simple habits that protect you.', 3, '/blog/covers/how-to-avoid-service-scams.webp', 'Illustration for the article: How to Spot and Avoid Common Service Scams' from public.blog_categories c where c.slug = 'finding-services' on conflict (slug) do nothing;
update public.blog_posts set cover_image_url = '/blog/covers/how-to-avoid-service-scams.webp', cover_image_alt = 'Illustration for the article: How to Spot and Avoid Common Service Scams' where slug = 'how-to-avoid-service-scams' and cover_image_url is null;
insert into public.blog_posts (slug, title, excerpt, content, category_id, author_name, status, published_at, seo_title, seo_description, reading_minutes, cover_image_url, cover_image_alt) select 'google-business-profile-guide', 'How to Get Your Business on Google Maps: A Google Business Profile Guide', 'A free Google Business Profile helps customers find you on Google Search and Maps. Here''s how to create, verify and optimise one — step by step.', $md$When someone searches for "plumber near me" or "dentist in Mahikeng", Google often shows a map with a short list of local businesses before any websites. Those listings come from **Google Business Profile** — a free tool that every local business in South Africa should set up.

This guide explains how to create your profile, get it verified and keep it working for you.

## Why your Google Business Profile matters

- **It's free.** There's no cost to create or maintain a profile.
- **It appears where customers look.** Your business can show up on Google Maps and in the local results on Google Search.
- **It answers questions quickly.** Customers see your hours, phone number, directions and photos without visiting a website.
- **It works alongside directories.** Listing on Google *and* on platforms like Findora gives customers more ways to find you, and consistent details across the web build trust.

## Step 1: Check whether a profile already exists

Search for your business name and town on Google Maps. A profile may already exist — created by Google from public information or by a previous owner. If it does, choose **Claim this business** rather than creating a duplicate.

## Step 2: Create your profile

Sign in with a Google account you'll keep long term (ideally a business account, not a staff member's personal email) and add your business. You'll be asked for:

1. **Business name** — your real trading name, exactly as customers know it. Don't add keywords or towns to the name; it can lead to suspension.
2. **Category** — choose the most specific primary category (for example "Dental clinic" rather than "Health").
3. **Location** — add your address if customers visit you. If you visit customers (like a mobile mechanic), you can hide your address and set a service area instead.
4. **Contact details** — phone number and website, if you have one.

## Step 3: Verify your business

Google needs to confirm you're the real owner before your profile goes fully live. Depending on your business, verification may happen by phone, email, video recording or another method Google offers. Follow the instructions carefully and be patient — verification can take a few days.

## Step 4: Complete every section

A complete profile performs better and looks more trustworthy:

- **Hours**, including special hours for public holidays.
- **Services or products**, with short descriptions and prices where appropriate.
- **Business description** — explain what you do, who you serve and where, in plain language.
- **Photos** — your logo, a cover photo, the outside and inside of your premises, your team and examples of your work.
- **Attributes** such as wheelchair accessibility or payment options, where available.

## Step 5: Keep your details consistent everywhere

Search engines compare your business information across websites. Make sure your name, address, phone number and hours match on Google, on your Findora profile, on social media and on your website. Inconsistent details can confuse both customers and search engines.

## Step 6: Encourage genuine reviews

Reviews influence both customers and local rankings. Ask happy customers to leave an honest review, and make it easy by sharing your review link. A few rules:

- Never buy reviews or write fake ones — it breaks Google's policies and can damage your reputation.
- Don't offer rewards in exchange for positive reviews.
- Reply politely to every review, including negative ones. A calm, helpful response shows future customers how you treat people.

## Step 7: Post updates and keep it fresh

You can share updates, offers and events on your profile. Regular activity shows customers you're active. Update your hours immediately when they change — few things frustrate customers more than arriving at a closed business that claims to be open.

## Common mistakes to avoid

- Keyword-stuffing your business name.
- Using a virtual office or P.O. Box address where customers can't visit.
- Creating duplicate profiles for the same location.
- Letting an ex-employee keep sole ownership of the profile.

## Bringing it together

Your Google Business Profile and your Findora listing work best together: Google helps people find you in map searches, while Findora lets customers browse by category, compare services and prices, and send you enquiries directly. Set up both, keep them accurate, and review them every few months.
$md$, c.id, 'Findora Editorial Team', 'published', now() - interval '24 days', 'How to Get Your Business on Google Maps: A Google Business Profile Gui', 'A free Google Business Profile helps customers find you on Google Search and Maps. Here''s how to create, verify and optimise one — step by step.', 3, '/blog/covers/google-business-profile-guide.webp', 'Illustration for the article: How to Get Your Business on Google Maps: A Google Business Profile Guide' from public.blog_categories c where c.slug = 'business-growth' on conflict (slug) do nothing;
update public.blog_posts set cover_image_url = '/blog/covers/google-business-profile-guide.webp', cover_image_alt = 'Illustration for the article: How to Get Your Business on Google Maps: A Google Business Profile Guide' where slug = 'google-business-profile-guide' and cover_image_url is null;
insert into public.blog_posts (slug, title, excerpt, content, category_id, author_name, status, published_at, seo_title, seo_description, reading_minutes, cover_image_url, cover_image_alt) select 'whatsapp-business-guide', 'WhatsApp Business for South African Small Businesses: A Practical Setup Guide', 'Most South African customers already use WhatsApp. Learn how to set up WhatsApp Business properly — profile, catalogue, quick replies, labels and good etiquette.', $md$For many South African customers, WhatsApp is the easiest way to contact a business. It's quick, familiar and works on almost any phone. The free **WhatsApp Business** app adds features designed for businesses, and setting it up properly can make you look more professional and save you hours every week.

## WhatsApp vs WhatsApp Business

The regular WhatsApp app is built for personal chats. WhatsApp Business is a separate free app that adds:

- A **business profile** with your address, hours, description, email and website.
- A **catalogue** to showcase products or services.
- **Quick replies** for answers you type often.
- **Greeting and away messages** sent automatically.
- **Labels** to organise chats (for example "New enquiry", "Quoted", "Paid").

Ideally, use a separate number for your business so personal and work conversations don't mix.

## Step 1: Complete your business profile

Fill in every field:

- **Business name** — the same name customers see on your signage and your Findora listing.
- **Category** — the closest match to what you do.
- **Description** — one or two sentences on what you offer and where.
- **Address and hours** — so customers know when to expect a reply.
- **Website or profile link** — you can link to your Findora business profile if you don't have a website.

Use your logo as the profile picture so customers instantly recognise you.

## Step 2: Build a simple catalogue

Add your main services or products with a photo, a short description and a price where possible. Customers can browse the catalogue before they message you, which means fewer repetitive questions and better-prepared enquiries.

## Step 3: Set up greeting and away messages

A **greeting message** welcomes new customers, for example:

> "Hi! Thanks for contacting Mokoena Plumbing. Please tell us your area and describe the problem — a photo helps. We reply within an hour during working hours."

An **away message** sets expectations after hours:

> "Thanks for your message. We're closed now and will reply from 7:30 tomorrow morning. For burst pipes, call 082 000 0000."

## Step 4: Create quick replies

Save answers to the questions you get most — prices, directions, payment options, booking information. Type "/" in a chat to insert them. Personalise each one with the customer's name before sending.

## Step 5: Organise chats with labels

Labels turn WhatsApp into a simple sales pipeline. A useful set:

- New enquiry
- Quote sent
- Booked
- Awaiting payment
- Completed

At a glance you'll know who needs a follow-up.

## Step 6: Share your WhatsApp link

WhatsApp lets you create a click-to-chat link. Put it on your social media, email signature and business cards. On Findora, adding your WhatsApp number shows a **WhatsApp** button on your profile so customers can message you in one tap — and your dashboard shows how many people used it.

## Good WhatsApp etiquette

- **Respect hours.** Avoid messaging customers late at night unless they asked you to.
- **Use text for important details.** Voice notes are convenient, but prices, dates and addresses should be written so they can be found later.
- **Don't add people to broadcast lists without permission.** Marketing messages require consent, and unwanted messages can get your number reported.
- **Keep it professional.** Friendly is good; slang and excessive emojis can undermine trust in some industries.
- **Protect personal information.** Don't forward customer details to others without a good reason.

## Measure what's working

Keep track of how many chats turn into paying jobs and how quickly you respond. Many businesses find that simply replying faster increases their conversion rate. Combine this with the contact statistics in your Findora dashboard to see which channels bring in the most customers.

## Final tip

WhatsApp Business is most powerful when it's part of a complete presence: a well-written directory profile, accurate Google listing and quick, friendly replies. Set it up once, keep it tidy, and it will quietly become one of your most valuable business tools.
$md$, c.id, 'Findora Editorial Team', 'published', now() - interval '21 days', 'WhatsApp Business for South African Small Businesses: A Practical Setu', 'Most South African customers already use WhatsApp. Learn how to set up WhatsApp Business properly — profile, catalogue, quick replies, labels and good etiquette.', 3, '/blog/covers/whatsapp-business-guide.webp', 'Illustration for the article: WhatsApp Business for South African Small Businesses: A Practical Setup Guide' from public.blog_categories c where c.slug = 'business-technology' on conflict (slug) do nothing;
update public.blog_posts set cover_image_url = '/blog/covers/whatsapp-business-guide.webp', cover_image_alt = 'Illustration for the article: WhatsApp Business for South African Small Businesses: A Practical Setup Guide' where slug = 'whatsapp-business-guide' and cover_image_url is null;
insert into public.blog_posts (slug, title, excerpt, content, category_id, author_name, status, published_at, seo_title, seo_description, reading_minutes, cover_image_url, cover_image_alt) select 'how-to-price-your-services', 'How to Price Your Services: A Practical Guide for Service Businesses', 'Pricing too low keeps you busy but broke; pricing too high scares customers away. A simple method for calculating prices that cover your costs and pay you fairly.', $md$Pricing is one of the hardest decisions for a new service business. Many owners copy a competitor's price or simply guess, then discover months later that they're working hard without making much money. A little structure makes pricing far less stressful.

## Start with your real costs

Before you can set a profitable price, you need to know what it costs to deliver your service. Split your costs into two groups.

**Direct costs** — things you spend for each job:

- Materials and consumables.
- Transport and fuel to and from the job.
- Any subcontractor or helper wages for that job.

**Overheads** — costs you pay whether or not you have work:

- Rent, electricity and water for your premises.
- Phone, data and software subscriptions.
- Insurance, accounting fees and bank charges.
- Equipment repairs and replacement.
- Marketing.

Add up your monthly overheads. This is the amount your work needs to cover before you make any profit.

## Decide what you need to earn

Next, decide what you need to pay yourself each month. Be realistic — include enough for your living costs and some savings. Your business isn't truly profitable if you can't pay yourself.

## Work out your billable hours

You can't bill every hour you work. Time spent travelling, quoting, buying materials, doing admin and marketing is usually unbilled. A common mistake is assuming 40 billable hours a week. Many small service businesses only bill 20 to 30.

Estimate honestly how many hours a month you can actually charge for.

## Calculate a minimum hourly rate

A simple formula:

> **Minimum hourly rate = (monthly overheads + what you need to earn) ÷ billable hours per month**

For example, if your overheads are R8,000, you need to earn R20,000, and you can bill 100 hours a month:

> (R8,000 + R20,000) ÷ 100 = **R280 per hour**

This is your *floor*, not your target. Charging less means you're losing money once you count everything. Add a profit margin on top so the business can grow, save for slow months and replace equipment.

## Then look at the market

Now compare your number with what similar businesses charge in your area. Browse business profiles and published prices, and ask suppliers or other business owners what's typical.

- If your calculated price is **far above** the market, look for ways to reduce costs or increase billable time, or target customers who value premium service.
- If it's **far below** the market, you may be underpricing — raise your prices.

## Choose a pricing model

Different services suit different models:

- **Fixed price** — clear and easy for customers, ideal for well-defined jobs (like a wheel alignment or a standard logo design).
- **Starting from** — shows a minimum while allowing for extra work.
- **Hourly rate** — suits work where the time needed is uncertain.
- **Quotation required** — for complex or custom jobs that need an assessment first.

On Findora you can choose a different price type for each service, so customers know what to expect before they contact you.

## Don't forget VAT

If your business is VAT-registered, make it clear whether your prices include VAT. Consumers generally expect prices to include VAT; business clients may prefer to see it separately. Ask your accountant how this applies to you.

## Communicate value, not just price

Customers compare more than numbers. Explain what's included, mention guarantees, qualifications and response times, and show photos of your work. A slightly higher price with clear value often wins over the cheapest option.

## Review your prices regularly

Costs rise every year. Review your prices at least annually and whenever a major cost (like fuel or materials) changes. Give existing customers notice of increases, and apply new prices to new quotes first.

## In summary

Know your costs, decide what you need to earn, be honest about billable time, calculate a minimum rate, compare with the market, and communicate your value clearly. Pricing confidently is one of the fastest ways to make a small business more sustainable.
$md$, c.id, 'Findora Editorial Team', 'published', now() - interval '18 days', 'How to Price Your Services: A Practical Guide for Service Businesses', 'Pricing too low keeps you busy but broke; pricing too high scares customers away. A simple method for calculating prices that cover your costs and pay you fairly.', 3, '/blog/covers/how-to-price-your-services.webp', 'Illustration for the article: How to Price Your Services: A Practical Guide for Service Businesses' from public.blog_categories c where c.slug = 'starting-a-business' on conflict (slug) do nothing;
